(*** Wolfram Notebook File ***)

Notebook[{

Cell["Understanding AI Text Watermarking Through Oil & Gas Operations", "Title"],

Cell["Real next-token probabilities from Qwen + simulated keyed statistical watermarking", "Subtitle"],

Cell["Educational simulation of keyed statistical text watermarking.
The next-token probabilities are generated by Qwen.
The watermarking logic shown here is simulated and does not reproduce Anthropic's proprietary implementation.", "Text"],

Cell["How this notebook is organized: Presentation Mode (below) tells the story in plain language with real data and minimal code. Technical Mode, further down, is collapsed by default -- click its section header to expand the full interactive machinery.", "Text"],

Cell["Setup", "Section"],

Cell["Run this section first. It imports the real Qwen data and defines the handful of shared functions used throughout the rest of the notebook.", "Text"],

Cell["dataFile = FileNameJoin[{NotebookDirectory[], \"..\", \"data\", \"processed\", \"next_token_distributions.json\"}];
fileReady = FileExistsQ[dataFile];
If[!fileReady,
  Print[Style[\"JSON file not found at: \" <> dataFile <>
    \"\\nPlease run notebooks/01_extract_next_token_probabilities.ipynb first, \" <>
    \"then re-evaluate this cell.\", Red, Bold]],
  qwenData = Import[dataFile, \"RawJSON\"];
  promptList = qwenData[\"prompts\"];
  promptIds = (#[\"prompt_id\"]) & /@ promptList;
  Print[\"Model: \", qwenData[\"metadata\"][\"model\"]];
  Print[\"Top-K: \", qwenData[\"metadata\"][\"top_k\"]];
  Print[\"Number of prompts: \", Length[promptList]];
  Print[\"Real example continuation available: \", KeyExistsQ[qwenData, \"generation\"]];
]", "Input"],

Cell["(* Renormalizes the top-k subset for sampling only -- the stored top-k probabilities
   themselves are NOT renormalized on export. *)
normalSample[candidates_] := Module[{tokens, probs, normProbs},
  tokens = (#[\"token\"]) & /@ candidates;
  probs = (#[\"probability\"]) & /@ candidates;
  normProbs = probs / Total[probs];
  RandomChoice[normProbs -> tokens]
];

(* Pedagogical keyed score: SHA256(key | context | token) mapped to a pseudorandom
   number in [0, 1]. A teaching construction, not a cryptographic watermarking scheme. *)
keyedScore[key_String, context_String, token_String] := Module[{hash, intVal},
  hash = Hash[key <> \"|\" <> context <> \"|\" <> token, \"SHA256\"];
  intVal = Mod[hash, 100000000];
  N[intVal / 100000000]
];

watermarkedDistribution[candidates_, key_String, context_String, strength_] := Module[
  {tokens, probs, normProbs, scores, centered, adjusted, total},
  tokens = (#[\"token\"]) & /@ candidates;
  probs = (#[\"probability\"]) & /@ candidates;
  normProbs = probs / Total[probs];
  scores = keyedScore[key, context, #] & /@ tokens;
  centered = scores - Mean[scores];
  adjusted = normProbs * Exp[strength * centered];
  total = Total[adjusted];
  AssociationThread[tokens -> (adjusted / total)]
];

(* Independent next-token decision simulations using imported oil & gas prompt
   contexts -- NOT full autoregressive generations. *)
simulateDecisions[n_Integer, key_String, strength_, mode_String] := Module[{decisions},
  decisions = Table[
    Module[{entry, drawn, dist},
      entry = RandomChoice[promptList];
      If[mode == \"normal\",
        drawn = normalSample[entry[\"candidates\"]],
        dist = watermarkedDistribution[entry[\"candidates\"], key, entry[\"prompt\"], strength];
        drawn = RandomChoice[Values[dist] -> Keys[dist]]
      ];
      keyedScore[key, entry[\"prompt\"], drawn]
    ],
    n
  ];
  Mean[decisions]
];", "Input"],

Cell["Presentation Mode", "Section"],

Cell["1. Why This Matters", "Subsection"],

Cell["An oil & gas professional does not need to know how text watermarking works to drill a well, design a completion, or run a CT intervention. The value here is understanding what happens when generative AI starts producing engineering documentation.", "Text"],

Cell["LLM-generated text can already show up almost anywhere technical writing happens:
• Daily drilling and completion reports
• End-of-well reports
• Well intervention summaries
• NPT descriptions
• Lessons learned
• Risk assessments and procedures
• Technical proposals and tender responses
• Knowledge-management systems and RAG-generated answers built on operational documents", "Text"],

Cell["Suppose an engineer asks an internal AI: \"Summarise the causes of NPT during the 8½-inch section.\" The resulting paragraph could eventually be copied into a report. Now questions arise about provenance: Was this written by an engineer? Generated by an AI? Generated and then reviewed? Can an organisation detect AI-generated material later? That is where watermarking becomes relevant -- and it is also where the rest of this notebook starts, from a real, computed answer to a simpler question first: what does an LLM actually do when it writes the next word of a technical sentence?", "Text"],

Cell["2. What You Are Seeing", "Subsection"],

Cell["Suppose an AI system is asked to write an oilfield operations note, and it produces the start of a sentence like: \"After reaching TD, the crew ...\". At almost every point in a sentence like this, more than one word is grammatically correct and operationally sensible. After \"the crew,\" a model might reasonably continue with \"circulated,\" \"pulled,\" \"prepared,\" or several other verbs. Ordinary language generation just samples from the model's own preferences among those choices.", "Text"],

Cell["A statistical watermark works by using a secret key to gently nudge which of those already-reasonable words gets picked -- without making the text worse, without inserting hidden characters, and without changing what is grammatically valid. A single word choice tells you almost nothing on its own. But across many word choices in a longer document, someone who knows the secret key can run a statistical test and determine -- probabilistically, never with certainty -- whether the text is more consistent with having come from the keyed (watermarked) process than from ordinary sampling.", "Text"],

Cell["The mental model that matters: watermarking does NOT work like this --
  normal text  ->  watermark algorithm  ->  modified text
It works like this instead --
  context  ->  LLM probability distribution  ->  key-dependent sampling bias  ->  next token  ->  new context  ->  repeat
The key never introduces a word the model hadn't already considered plausible. It only shifts which already-plausible word is more or less likely to be sampled. The engineering knowledge has not changed -- only the sampling behaviour has. Sections 5 and 6 below show this one real decision at a time, using Qwen's actual probabilities -- never a before/after rewrite of a finished sentence.", "Text"],

Cell["This notebook shows you three real, computed things, and one simulated thing:
REAL -- the actual next-word probabilities Qwen (an open-weight AI model) assigns at several points in oilfield operational sentences.
REAL -- one actual multi-word continuation Qwen generated on its own.
SIMULATED -- a simple, transparent, teaching version of a keyed watermark, applied to those real probabilities.
SIMULATED -- a matching statistical detector for that simulated watermark.

None of this reproduces Anthropic's actual Claude watermarking system, its keys, or its detector.", "Text"],

Cell["3. Glossary", "Subsection"],

Cell["Grid[
  {
    {Style[\"Token\", Bold], \"A chunk of text an LLM works with -- often a word, part of a word, or a punctuation mark, not always a full English word.\"},
    {Style[\"Probability\", Bold], \"How strongly the model prefers each possible next token, computed by the model for the current context.\"},
    {Style[\"Secret key\", Bold], \"A password-like string that determines how the simulated watermark nudges word choices. Different keys nudge choices differently.\"},
    {Style[\"Watermark\", Bold], \"In this simulator, a way of preferring certain already-plausible word choices over others, based on a secret key and the surrounding context -- never a way of choosing an implausible word.\"},
    {Style[\"Detector\", Bold], \"A process that checks a stretch of text against a candidate key, looking for a statistical pattern showing word choices are more consistent with a watermarked (keyed) process than with plain, unwatermarked sampling.\"},
    {Style[\"Z-score\", Bold], \"A statistic measuring how many standard deviations an observed result is from what would be expected by chance. A larger |z| means the result would be more surprising if there were actually no watermark.\"}
  },
  Frame -> All, Background -> {None, {LightGray, None}}, Alignment -> Left,
  ItemSize -> {{12, 60}}, Spacings -> {2, 1}
]", "Input"],

Cell["4. How Watermarking Works: A Diagram", "Subsection"],

Cell["This diagram summarizes the pipeline this notebook implements, from a real prompt all the way to a simulated statistical detector.", "Text"],

Cell["diagramVertices = {
  \"Oil & gas\\nprompt\", \"Qwen\\n(real LLM)\", \"Real next-token\\nprobabilities\",
  \"Secret key +\\ncontext\", \"Simulated\\nreweighting\", \"Sampled\\nword\",
  \"Repeat for many\\nwords\", \"Detector /\\nstatistical score\"
};
diagramEdges = Table[diagramVertices[[i]] -> diagramVertices[[i + 1]], {i, Length[diagramVertices] - 1}];
Graph[diagramEdges,
  VertexLabels -> \"Name\",
  VertexLabelStyle -> Directive[Bold, 11],
  VertexShapeFunction -> \"RoundedRectangle\",
  VertexSize -> {0.7, 0.35},
  GraphLayout -> \"LayeredDigraphEmbedding\",
  EdgeStyle -> Directive[Gray, Thick],
  ImageSize -> Large,
  PlotLabel -> \"How this simulator works, step by step\"
]", "Input"],

Cell["5. One Real Token Decision, Explained", "Subsection"],

Cell["Pick a real oilfield prompt below. The table shows Qwen's actual candidate words at that exact decision point, each one's real probability, its pseudorandom key score, and the resulting simulated watermarked probability. \"Normal selection\" is the candidate Qwen itself ranks highest. \"Watermarked selection\" is the candidate the simulated key ranks highest. Both are drawn from the SAME real candidate list -- the key never adds a new word.", "Text"],

Cell["oneDecisionTable[candidates_, key_String, context_String, strength_] := Module[
  {tokens, normProbs, keyScores, wDist, wProbsOrdered, rows},
  tokens = (#[\"token\"]) & /@ candidates;
  normProbs = (#[\"probability\"]) & /@ candidates;
  normProbs = normProbs / Total[normProbs];
  keyScores = keyedScore[key, context, #] & /@ tokens;
  wDist = watermarkedDistribution[candidates, key, context, strength];
  wProbsOrdered = wDist[#] & /@ tokens;
  rows = Table[
    {tokens[[i]],
     ToString[NumberForm[100 normProbs[[i]], {3, 1}]] <> \"%\",
     ToString[NumberForm[keyScores[[i]], {4, 3}]],
     ToString[NumberForm[100 wProbsOrdered[[i]], {3, 1}]] <> \"%\"},
    {i, Length[tokens]}
  ];
  Grid[
    Prepend[rows, {\"Candidate\", \"Normal probability\", \"Key score\", \"Watermarked probability\"}],
    Frame -> All, Background -> {None, {LightGray, None}}, Alignment -> Center
  ]
];

Manipulate[
  Module[{entry, normalPick, wDist, watermarkPick},
    entry = SelectFirst[promptList, #[\"prompt_id\"] == promptId &];
    normalPick = entry[\"candidates\"][[1]][\"token\"];
    wDist = watermarkedDistribution[entry[\"candidates\"], key, entry[\"prompt\"], strength];
    watermarkPick = First[Keys[ReverseSort[wDist]]];
    Column[{
      Style[\"Context: \\\"\" <> entry[\"prompt\"] <> \" ...\\\"\", Bold],
      oneDecisionTable[entry[\"candidates\"], key, entry[\"prompt\"], strength],
      Row[{Style[\"Normal selection: \", Bold], normalPick}],
      Row[{Style[\"Watermarked selection: \", Bold], watermarkPick}],
      Style[
        \"The key did not introduce the word \\\"\" <> watermarkPick <>
        \"\\\". Qwen already considered it plausible. The watermark only changed its sampling preference.\",
        Italic
      ]
    }]
  ],
  {{promptId, First[promptIds], \"Prompt\"}, promptIds},
  {{key, \"8F3A91C7\", \"Secret key\"}, InputField[#, String] &},
  {{strength, 0.5, \"Watermark strength\"}, 0, 2}
]", "Input"],

Cell["6. A Sequence of Real Decisions", "Subsection"],

Cell["This table replays 8 real, consecutive decision points from one actual Qwen continuation (see Section 7 of the Python notebook) -- the same real context Qwen actually saw at each step. For each step it shows the token Qwen itself chose (\"Normal token\") next to the token the simulated watermark would have preferred at that same real step (\"Watermarked token\"). This is deliberately shown as a table of independent decisions, not as two competing sentences -- watermarking does not rewrite finished text; it biases one sampling decision at a time.", "Text"],

Cell["sequenceTable[genData_, key_String, strength_] := Module[
  {steps, rows, normalScores, watermarkScores},
  steps = genData[\"steps\"];
  rows = Table[
    Module[{step = steps[[i]], normalToken, wDist, watermarkToken, consistent, contextShown},
      normalToken = step[\"chosen_token\"];
      wDist = watermarkedDistribution[step[\"candidates\"], key, step[\"context_before\"], strength];
      watermarkToken = First[Keys[ReverseSort[wDist]]];
      consistent = If[normalToken == watermarkToken, \"yes\", \"no\"];
      contextShown = If[StringLength[step[\"context_before\"]] > 45,
        \"...\" <> StringTake[step[\"context_before\"], -42],
        step[\"context_before\"]
      ];
      {i, contextShown, normalToken, watermarkToken, consistent}
    ],
    {i, Length[steps]}
  ];
  normalScores = Table[
    keyedScore[key, steps[[i]][\"context_before\"], steps[[i]][\"chosen_token\"]],
    {i, Length[steps]}
  ];
  watermarkScores = Table[
    Module[{wDist = watermarkedDistribution[steps[[i]][\"candidates\"], key, steps[[i]][\"context_before\"], strength]},
      keyedScore[key, steps[[i]][\"context_before\"], First[Keys[ReverseSort[wDist]]]]
    ],
    {i, Length[steps]}
  ];
  Column[{
    Grid[
      Prepend[rows, {\"Step\", \"Context (end)\", \"Normal token\", \"Watermarked token\", \"Key-consistent?\"}],
      Frame -> All, Background -> {None, {LightGray, None}}, Alignment -> Left
    ],
    Style[
      \"Mean keyed score -- normal: \" <> ToString[NumberForm[Mean[normalScores], {4, 3}]] <>
      \"   simulated watermark: \" <> ToString[NumberForm[Mean[watermarkScores], {4, 3}]] <>
      \"   (expected under no watermark: about 0.500)\",
      Bold
    ]
  }]
];

Manipulate[
  sequenceTable[qwenData[\"generation\"], sKey, sStrength],
  {{sKey, \"8F3A91C7\", \"Secret key\"}, InputField[#, String] &},
  {{sStrength, 0, \"Watermark strength\"}, 0, 2}
]", "Input"],

Cell["A word is not inherently watermarked. Its preference depends on the secret key and the preceding context together -- change either one, and a different word may become preferred.", "Text"],

Cell["7. Technical Language Constrains Token Choice", "Subsection"],

Cell["Compare \"It was a pleasant afternoon, and the children decided to ...\" with \"Pressure test the tubing to 5,000 psi for 15 ...\". The second sentence has far less freedom: changing the unit, the number, or the operation would change what the report actually says, not just how it says it. This section measures that difference directly, using real Qwen probabilities for both prompts -- no invented numbers.", "Text"],

Cell["concentrationStats[candidates_] := Module[{probs, normProbs, top1, entropyBits},
  probs = (#[\"probability\"]) & /@ candidates;
  normProbs = probs / Total[probs];
  top1 = Max[normProbs];
  entropyBits = -Total[normProbs * Log2[normProbs]];
  <|\"top1\" -> top1, \"entropyBits\" -> entropyBits|>
];

Module[{genericEntry, techEntry, genericStats, techStats},
  genericEntry = SelectFirst[promptList, #[\"prompt_id\"] == \"PGEN\" &];
  techEntry = SelectFirst[promptList, #[\"prompt_id\"] == \"PTECH\" &];
  genericStats = concentrationStats[genericEntry[\"candidates\"]];
  techStats = concentrationStats[techEntry[\"candidates\"]];
  Column[{
    Row[{
      Column[{
        Style[\"Generic: \\\"\" <> genericEntry[\"prompt\"] <> \" ...\\\"\", Bold],
        BarChart[
          (#[\"probability\"]) & /@ genericEntry[\"candidates\"],
          ChartLabels -> Placed[(#[\"token\"]) & /@ genericEntry[\"candidates\"], Axis, Rotate[#, Pi/3] &],
          PlotLabel -> \"top-1 probability: \" <> ToString[NumberForm[100 genericStats[\"top1\"], {3, 1}]] <> \"%\",
          ImageSize -> Medium, ChartStyle -> \"Pastel\"
        ]
      }],
      Spacer[30],
      Column[{
        Style[\"Technical: \\\"\" <> techEntry[\"prompt\"] <> \" ...\\\"\", Bold],
        BarChart[
          (#[\"probability\"]) & /@ techEntry[\"candidates\"],
          ChartLabels -> Placed[(#[\"token\"]) & /@ techEntry[\"candidates\"], Axis, Rotate[#, Pi/3] &],
          PlotLabel -> \"top-1 probability: \" <> ToString[NumberForm[100 techStats[\"top1\"], {3, 1}]] <> \"%\",
          ImageSize -> Medium, ChartStyle -> \"Pastel\"
        ]
      }]
    }],
    Grid[{
      {\"\", \"Top-1 probability\", \"Entropy (bits, lower = more constrained)\"},
      {\"Generic\", ToString[NumberForm[100 genericStats[\"top1\"], {3, 1}]] <> \"%\", ToString[NumberForm[genericStats[\"entropyBits\"], {3, 2}]]},
      {\"Technical\", ToString[NumberForm[100 techStats[\"top1\"], {3, 1}]] <> \"%\", ToString[NumberForm[techStats[\"entropyBits\"], {3, 2}]]}
    }, Frame -> All, Background -> {None, {LightGray, None}}, Alignment -> Center],
    Style[
      \"The technical prompt is far more concentrated on one candidate than the generic \" <>
      \"one. That concentration is exactly the quantity a simulated watermark has to work \" <>
      \"with -- when nearly all the real probability mass sits on a single word (here, a \" <>
      \"unit of time), the key has very little room to prefer an alternative without \" <>
      \"reaching into candidates Qwen considered far less plausible, which is more likely \" <>
      \"to visibly change the engineering meaning of the sentence.\",
      Italic
    ]
  }]
]", "Input"],

Cell["8. Common Misconceptions", "Subsection"],

Cell["\"No hidden text is inserted.\" The watermark does not add secret characters, invisible text, or metadata. It only nudges which of several already-plausible words gets chosen.", "Item"],

Cell["\"A word is not permanently watermarked.\" The same word can be watermark-preferred in one context and not in another -- preference depends on the key and the surrounding context together (see the context-dependence table in Technical Mode).", "Item"],

Cell["\"Detection is probabilistic.\" A single word, or even a single sentence, proves nothing on its own. Confidence builds up statistically only after observing many independent word choices.", "Item"],

Cell["\"Short text may not contain enough signal.\" A short passage may not contain enough word-choice decisions for the statistical signal to separate from ordinary random variation (see the signal-accumulation chart in Technical Mode).", "Item"],

Cell["9. Plain-Language Score Interpretation", "Subsection"],

Cell["A z-score is a number -- useful for the math, but not very intuitive on its own. This section maps a demo z-score onto a plain-language sentence instead. The thresholds below are specific to this teaching simulator; they are illustrative, not real detection thresholds used by any production watermarking system.", "Text"],

Cell["plainLanguageVerdict[z_] := Which[
  Abs[z] < 1, \"This looks like ordinary, unwatermarked text -- no meaningful statistical signal.\",
  Abs[z] < 2, \"This shows a weak statistical signal -- consistent with either watermarked or unwatermarked text.\",
  Abs[z] < 3, \"This shows a moderate statistical signal, more consistent with watermarked text than chance alone.\",
  True, \"This shows a strong statistical signal, unlikely to occur by chance under normal (unwatermarked) sampling.\"
];

Module[{nullStats, nullMean, nullSD, observedMean, z},
  nullStats = Table[simulateDecisions[150, \"8F3A91C7\", 0.5, \"normal\"], 150];
  nullMean = Mean[nullStats];
  nullSD = StandardDeviation[nullStats];
  observedMean = simulateDecisions[150, \"8F3A91C7\", 0.5, \"watermarked\"];
  z = (observedMean - nullMean) / nullSD;
  Column[{
    \"Numeric z-score: \" <> ToString[NumberForm[z, {5, 4}]],
    Style[\"Plain-language reading: \" <> plainLanguageVerdict[z], Bold],
    Style[\"This is specific to this teaching simulator and is not Anthropic's detector or threshold.\", Italic, Red]
  }]
]", "Input"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Technical Mode -- Interactive Machinery", "Section"],

Cell["This section is collapsed by default. Expand it to see the full underlying Wolfram Language code: interactive prompt/probability exploration, the simulated keyed watermark exercised in more detail, context and strength sensitivity, statistical signal accumulation, a Monte Carlo experiment, an optional z-score detector, an oil & gas worked example, limitations, and reproducibility. It reuses the functions and data already loaded in the Setup section above -- nothing here is redefined.", "Text"],

Cell["1. Prompt selector", "Subsection"],

Cell["Manipulate[
  Module[{entry = SelectFirst[promptList, #[\"prompt_id\"] == selectedId &]},
    Column[{
      Style[\"Prompt: \" <> entry[\"prompt\"], Bold, 14],
      \"Input token count: \" <> ToString[entry[\"input_token_count\"]],
      Grid[
        Prepend[
          ({#[\"rank\"], #[\"token\"], #[\"probability\"]} & /@ entry[\"candidates\"]),
          {\"Rank\", \"Token\", \"Probability\"}
        ],
        Frame -> All, Background -> {None, {LightGray, None}}
      ]
    }]
  ],
  {{selectedId, First[promptIds], \"Prompt\"}, promptIds}
]", "Input"],

Cell["2. Real next-token probability chart", "Subsection"],

Cell["These are the actual Qwen next-token probabilities imported in Setup -- not Claude probabilities, and not yet watermarked in any way.", "Text"],

Cell["plotRealDistribution[promptId_] := Module[{entry, tokens, probs},
  entry = SelectFirst[promptList, #[\"prompt_id\"] == promptId &];
  tokens = (#[\"token\"]) & /@ entry[\"candidates\"];
  probs = (#[\"probability\"]) & /@ entry[\"candidates\"];
  BarChart[probs,
    ChartLabels -> Placed[tokens, Axis, Rotate[#, Pi/3] &],
    PlotLabel -> \"Actual Qwen next-token probabilities\\nPrompt: \" <> entry[\"prompt\"],
    AxesLabel -> {None, \"Probability\"},
    ImageSize -> Large,
    ChartStyle -> \"Pastel\"
  ]
];

Manipulate[
  plotRealDistribution[promptId],
  {{promptId, First[promptIds], \"Prompt\"}, promptIds}
]", "Input"],

Cell["3. Normal sampling", "Subsection"],

Cell["The original exported top-k probabilities are not renormalized. For sampling only, normalSample (defined in Setup) renormalizes the displayed top-k subset.", "Text"],

Cell["Manipulate[
  Module[{entry, draws},
    entry = SelectFirst[promptList, #[\"prompt_id\"] == promptId &];
    draws = Table[normalSample[entry[\"candidates\"]], nDraws];
    Column[{
      Style[\"Prompt: \" <> entry[\"prompt\"], Bold],
      Grid[
        Prepend[Transpose[{Range[Length[draws]], draws}], {\"Draw #\", \"Sampled Token\"}],
        Frame -> All
      ]
    }]
  ],
  {{promptId, First[promptIds], \"Prompt\"}, promptIds},
  {{nDraws, 10, \"Number of draws\"}, {10, 20}}
]", "Input"],

Cell["4. Context dependence", "Subsection"],

Cell["contextDependenceTable = Table[
  Module[{wDist, bestToken, bestProb, sorted},
    wDist = watermarkedDistribution[entry[\"candidates\"], \"8F3A91C7\", entry[\"prompt\"], 0.5];
    sorted = ReverseSort[wDist];
    bestToken = First[Keys[sorted]];
    bestProb = First[Values[sorted]];
    {entry[\"prompt\"], bestToken, NumberForm[bestProb, {5, 4}]}
  ],
  {entry, promptList}
];

Grid[
  Prepend[contextDependenceTable, {\"Prompt\", \"Highest weighted token\", \"Probability\"}],
  Frame -> All, Background -> {None, {LightGray, None}}
]", "Input"],

Cell["The preferred token under the same secret key changes as the prompt context changes -- the keyed score depends on context, not just the token string alone.", "Text"],

Cell["5. Statistical signal accumulation", "Subsection"],

Cell["Independent next-token decision simulations using imported oil & gas prompt contexts. These are NOT full autoregressive text generations -- each simulated decision independently picks a random imported prompt context and draws one next token.", "Text"],

Cell["sampleSizes = {20, 50, 100, 200, 500, 1000};
accumNormalMeans = simulateDecisions[#, \"8F3A91C7\", 0.5, \"normal\"] & /@ sampleSizes;
accumWatermarkedMeans = simulateDecisions[#, \"8F3A91C7\", 0.5, \"watermarked\"] & /@ sampleSizes;

ListLinePlot[
  {Transpose[{sampleSizes, accumNormalMeans}], Transpose[{sampleSizes, accumWatermarkedMeans}]},
  PlotLegends -> {\"Normal sampling\", \"Simulated watermark sampling\"},
  AxesLabel -> {\"Number of token decisions (N)\", \"Mean keyed score\"},
  PlotLabel -> \"Statistical signal accumulation\",
  PlotMarkers -> Automatic,
  ImageSize -> Large
]", "Input"],

Cell["As N grows, the mean keyed score for normal sampling settles near 0.5 (the expected value of a pseudorandom score with no key preference), while the simulated watermarked mean settles above it. The gap becomes more statistically stable as more independent observations accumulate -- the same reason opinion polls get more reliable with more respondents, not because any single response becomes more certain.", "Text"],

Cell["6. Monte Carlo experiment", "Subsection"],

Cell["Manipulate[
  Module[{normalStats, watermarkedStats},
    normalStats = Table[simulateDecisions[nDecisions, mcKey, mcStrength, \"normal\"], nTrials];
    watermarkedStats = Table[simulateDecisions[nDecisions, mcKey, mcStrength, \"watermarked\"], nTrials];
    Row[{
      Histogram[normalStats, PlotLabel -> \"Normal sampling statistic\", ImageSize -> Medium, AxesLabel -> {\"Mean keyed score\", \"Count\"}],
      Spacer[20],
      Histogram[watermarkedStats, PlotLabel -> \"Simulated watermark statistic\", ImageSize -> Medium, AxesLabel -> {\"Mean keyed score\", \"Count\"}]
    }]
  ],
  {{nDecisions, 100, \"N token decisions\"}, {50, 100, 200}},
  {{nTrials, 50, \"Number of trials\"}, {20, 50, 100}},
  {{mcKey, \"8F3A91C7\", \"Secret key\"}, InputField[#, String] &},
  {{mcStrength, 0.5, \"Strength\"}, 0, 2}
]", "Input"],

Cell["This compares the DISTRIBUTION of the mean keyed score across many repeated trials, not just a single run -- illustrating how separated the normal and watermarked statistics become under repeated sampling. This does not claim any real-world detection threshold.", "Text"],

Cell["7. Optional z-score detector", "Subsection"],

Cell["Module[{nullStats, nullMean, nullSD, observedMean, z},
  nullStats = Table[simulateDecisions[200, \"8F3A91C7\", 0.5, \"normal\"], 200];
  nullMean = Mean[nullStats];
  nullSD = StandardDeviation[nullStats];
  observedMean = simulateDecisions[200, \"8F3A91C7\", 0.5, \"watermarked\"];
  z = (observedMean - nullMean) / nullSD;
  Column[{
    \"Observed mean (simulated watermarked sample): \" <> ToString[NumberForm[observedMean, {5, 4}]],
    \"Null mean (normal sampling, N=200 trials): \" <> ToString[NumberForm[nullMean, {5, 4}]],
    \"Null SD: \" <> ToString[NumberForm[nullSD, {5, 4}]],
    Style[\"z-score: \" <> ToString[NumberForm[z, {5, 4}]], Bold],
    Style[\"This z-score is specific to this simulator and is not Anthropic's detector.\", Italic, Red]
  }]
]", "Input"],

Cell["8. Key sensitivity", "Subsection"],

Cell["Module[{entry, distA, distB},
  entry = promptList[[1]];
  distA = watermarkedDistribution[entry[\"candidates\"], \"8F3A91C7\", entry[\"prompt\"], 0.5];
  distB = watermarkedDistribution[entry[\"candidates\"], \"AB71D204\", entry[\"prompt\"], 0.5];
  Row[{
    Column[{Style[\"Key: 8F3A91C7\", Bold], BarChart[Values[distA], ChartLabels -> Placed[Keys[distA], Axis, Rotate[#, Pi/3] &], ImageSize -> Medium]}],
    Spacer[30],
    Column[{Style[\"Key: AB71D204\", Bold], BarChart[Values[distB], ChartLabels -> Placed[Keys[distB], Axis, Rotate[#, Pi/3] &], ImageSize -> Medium]}]
  }]
]", "Input"],

Cell["Changing the secret key produces a different simulated watermark distribution over the same real Qwen candidates -- the reweighting is entirely determined by the key, context, and token together.", "Text"],

Cell["9. Strength sensitivity", "Subsection"],

Cell["Module[{entry, strengths, dists},
  entry = promptList[[1]];
  strengths = {0.0, 0.1, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 1.0};
  dists = watermarkedDistribution[entry[\"candidates\"], \"8F3A91C7\", entry[\"prompt\"], #] & /@ strengths;
  GraphicsGrid[
    Partition[
      Table[
        BarChart[Values[dists[[i]]],
          ChartLabels -> Placed[Keys[dists[[i]]], Axis, Rotate[#, Pi/3] &],
          PlotLabel -> \"strength = \" <> ToString[strengths[[i]]],
          ImageSize -> Small
        ],
        {i, Length[strengths]}
      ],
      3
    ],
    ImageSize -> Full
  ]
]", "Input"],

Cell["At strength = 0, the simulated distribution is identical to the normal (renormalized) Qwen distribution -- the keyed score has no effect. As strength increases, the reweighting toward key-preferred tokens becomes progressively stronger. No particular strength value shown here corresponds to any value used by Anthropic or any other production watermarking system.", "Text"],

Cell["10. Oil & gas interpretation: a worked example", "Subsection"],

Cell["This section pulls a real prompt and its real top candidates directly from the imported JSON -- nothing here is hardcoded or invented.", "Text"],

Cell["Module[{entry, topTokens},
  entry = SelectFirst[promptList, #[\"prompt_id\"] == \"P03\" &];
  topTokens = StringRiffle[(#[\"token\"]) & /@ entry[\"candidates\"][[1 ;; 4]], \", \"];
  Column[{
    \"Prompt: \\\"\" <> entry[\"prompt\"] <> \"\\\"\",
    \"Real top-4 next-word candidates from Qwen: \" <> topTokens,
    \"\",
    \"Under normal (unwatermarked) sampling, Qwen's own probabilities determine which \" <>
     \"of these plausible words is more or less likely. The simulated secret key can \" <>
     \"shift preference among these plausible candidates without making any of them \" <>
     \"implausible or grammatically wrong -- the watermark rides on the natural \" <>
     \"ambiguity of word choice that already exists in technical writing. Across many \" <>
     \"such decisions in a long document, a detector who knows the same key can test \" <>
     \"whether the sequence of choices is more key-consistent than expected under \" <>
     \"normal, unwatermarked sampling -- exactly what the signal-accumulation and \" <>
     \"Monte Carlo sections above simulate.\"
  }]
]", "Input"],

Cell["11. Limitations", "Subsection"],

Cell["Qwen probabilities are used here, not Claude probabilities.", "Item"],

Cell["The top-k candidates represent only part of the full vocabulary distribution.", "Item"],

Cell["The watermark transformation shown here is simulated, built for this project.", "Item"],

Cell["Token decisions in the statistical sections are simplified, independent draws -- not full autoregressive generation (Section 6 in Presentation Mode uses real, consecutive decision points from one real generation, but each row's watermarked pick is computed independently against the real context at that step, not chained into a new generation).", "Item"],

Cell["This experiment does not reproduce any production watermark detection system.", "Item"],

Cell["Real watermark systems may use different context windows, scoring functions, keys, sampling rules, and detector statistics than shown here.", "Item"],

Cell["Editing, paraphrasing, short text length, constrained technical text, and code can all affect real-world detectability in ways this simulator does not model -- Section 7 in Presentation Mode quantifies one specific instance of this (numeric/unit-constrained text) using real data.", "Item"],

Cell["12. Reproducibility", "Subsection"],

Cell["Column[{
  \"Model: \" <> qwenData[\"metadata\"][\"model\"],
  \"JSON file: \" <> dataFile,
  \"Number of prompts: \" <> ToString[Length[promptList]],
  \"Top-K: \" <> ToString[qwenData[\"metadata\"][\"top_k\"]],
  \"Secret key used in this demo: 8F3A91C7\",
  \"Watermark strength used in this demo: 0.5\",
  \"\",
  \"To regenerate the JSON: rerun notebooks/01_extract_next_token_probabilities.ipynb, then re-evaluate this notebook from the top.\"
}]", "Input"]

}, Closed]]

},
WindowSize -> {1100, 900},
WindowMargins -> {{100, Automatic}, {Automatic, 50}},
FrontEndVersion -> "14.0 for Mac OS X",
